./data/screening_codex_fulltext_docling_graph_direct_clean_both_targets_2026-07-10/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0020.stderr.log:104:[{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001313","candidate_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001313","source_record_id":"rec_001313","source_corpus":"uncertain","title":"PlantBiMoE: A Bidirectional Foundation Model with Sparsemoe for Plant Genomes","abstract":"","doi":"10.1109/BIBM66473.2025.11356776","year":"","venue":"","sources":[],"selected_full_text_sections":"[data_source, input_representation: II. MATERIALS AND METHODS > 1) A pre-training dataset comprising genomic sequences from 42 plant species]\nTo train PlantBiMoE, we constructed a high-quality pretraining dataset of plant genomes, which covers a total of 42 plant species in six broad categories: model plants, vegetables, fruits, cereals, algae and other important plants, as shown in Fig. 2. For each species, we obtained its reference genome FNA file from NCBI and performed the following data preprocessing sequentially:\n\n- The reference genomes of all plants were cut into segments with a fixed length of 32,768 bp. An overlap strategy was introduced during the cut into segments to do implicit data enhancement, i.e., an overlap interval of 64 to 128 bp was retained between two neighboring segments, and the sliding step size was randomly sampled within this range.\n- Replace all non-standard bases in the fragments, other than A, T, C, G, and N, with N uniformly.\n- Filter out all sequences with more than 2% of base N to form the initial dataset.\n- Randomly select 30% of the sequences in the initial dataset for reverse complementation (RC) and add them to the initial dataset to form the final dataset.\n\nFinally, we obtained a dataset containing 25.40B nucleic acid pairs. We divided the dataset into a training set and a test set according to chromosomes, with 5% of the test set, which ensures the relative independence of the training and validation sets in terms of species and chromosomes. This strategy helps to improve the generalization assessment validity of the model in the validation stage.","first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001313","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"A new foundation model is presented for plant genomes, with pretraining on reference genome sequences from 42 plant species. The provided evidence contains only nucleic acid sequences and no natural-language text component or text-bio bridge.","evidence_snippet":"\"pretraining dataset of plant genomes\"; \"reference genomes of all plants were cut into segments\"; \"25.40B nucleic acid pairs\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001313","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The record describes a pretraining foundation model on genomes from 42 plant species and emphasizes generalization across species/chromosomes. It does not clearly state a generative mechanism such as decoder, autoregressive, sequence-to-sequence, or diffusion.","evidence_snippet":"“A pre-training dataset comprising genomic sequences from 42 plant species” and “This strategy helps to improve the generalization assessment validity of the model”"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}}]
