./data/screening_codex_fulltext_2026-07-09/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0070.stderr.log:109:[{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_003208","candidate_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_003208","source_record_id":"rec_003208","source_corpus":"uncertain","title":"Multi-Scale AI-Assisted Gene Expression Decoding","abstract":"Genes can be treated as a graph that can be mapped. Tremendous information is coded in genes to form a complex organism. Decoding this information is critical to understanding our biology and developing treatments for various diseases including cancer. Deep learning, a new branch of computer science, has gained traction over the past decade. Our study has shown that deep-learning models can be an effective tool in decoding genetic data such as gene tissue-deconvolution, gene graph mapping and genomic imputation. In tasks such as tissue deconvolution, our research has demonstrated the superior capability of deep learning-based approaches in capturing sample variations compared to traditional numerical analytical methods. While our approach requires large relevant datasets for effective deep learning training, this challenge can be addressed with increasing data availability. In gene graphing and mapping, our Graph Neural Network based approach consistently outperforms traditional regression techniques by a significant margin. The primary challenge here lies in the demand for substantial computing resources; however, the ongoing growth in average computing power and the enhanced accessibility of computational resources are expected to alleviate this constraint over time. Moreover, in the realm of generating and imputing missing biological data, cutting-edge generative AI models have proven to be invaluable. [truncated]","doi":"","year":"","venue":"","sources":[],"full_text_context":"[abstract: ABSTRACT]\nGenes can be treated as a graph that can be mapped. Tremendous information is coded in genes to form a complex organism. Decoding this information is critical to understanding our biology and developing treatments for various diseases including cancer. Deep learning, a new branch of computer science, has gained traction over the past decade. Our study has shown that deep-learning models can be an effective tool in decoding genetic data such as gene tissue-deconvolution, gene graph mapping and genomic imputation. In tasks such as tissue deconvolution, our research has demonstrated the superior capability of deep learning-based approaches in capturing sample variations compared to traditional numerical analytical methods. While our approach requires large relevant datasets for effective deep learning training, this challenge can be addressed with increasing data availability. In gene graphing and mapping, our Graph Neural Network based approach consistently outperforms traditional regression techniques by a significant margin. The primary challenge here lies in the demand for substantial computing resources; however, the ongoing growth in average computing power and the enhanced accessibility of computational resources are expected to alleviate this constraint over time. Moreover, in the realm of generating and imputing missing biological data, cutting-edge generative AI models have proven to be invaluable. [truncated]\n\n[introduction: INTRODUCTION]\nmechanisms, and facilitating refined analyses for improved biological insights . In terms of methylation imputation, deep generative models, such as the Diffusion Denoise Probabilistic Model (DDPM), holds the potential to accurately infer missing methylation patterns, shedding light on epigenetic alterations underlying various physiological and pathological processes. By harnessing the power of these cutting-edge technologies, researchers are poised to unlock deeper insights into the molecular intricacies of biological systems, ultimately paving the way for transformative advances in personalized medicine and targeted therapeutics.","section_evidence":[{"section_type":"abstract","heading":"ABSTRACT","heading_level":2,"line_start":27,"line_end":32,"text":"Genes can be treated as a graph that can be mapped. Tremendous information is coded in genes to form a complex organism. Decoding this information is critical to understanding our biology and developing treatments for various diseases including cancer. Deep learning, a new branch of computer science, has gained traction over the past decade. Our study has shown that deep-learning models can be an effective tool in decoding genetic data such as gene tissue-deconvolution, gene graph mapping and genomic imputation. In tasks such as tissue deconvolution, our research has demonstrated the superior capability of deep learning-based approaches in capturing sample variations compared to traditional numerical analytical methods. While our approach requires large relevant datasets for effective deep learning training, this challenge can be addressed with increasing data availability. In gene graphing and mapping, our Graph Neural Network based approach consistently outperforms traditional regression techniques by a significant margin. The primary challenge here lies in the demand for substantial computing resources; however, the ongoing growth in average computing power and the enhanced accessibility of computational resources are expected to alleviate this constraint over time. Moreover, in the realm of generating and imputing missing biological data, cutting-edge generative AI models have proven to be invaluable. [truncated]","original_chars":2218},{"section_type":"introduction","heading":"INTRODUCTION","heading_level":2,"line_start":80,"line_end":83,"text":"mechanisms, and facilitating refined analyses for improved biological insights . In terms of methylation imputation, deep generative models, such as the Diffusion Denoise Probabilistic Model (DDPM), holds the potential to accurately infer missing methylation patterns, shedding light on epigenetic alterations underlying various physiological and pathological processes. By harnessing the power of these cutting-edge technologies, researchers are poised to unlock deeper insights into the molecular intricacies of biological systems, ultimately paving the way for transformative advances in personalized medicine and targeted therapeutics.","original_chars":639}],"docling_markdown":"data/docling_uncertain_manual_downloads_no_vlm_2026-07-08/markdown/rec_003208_multi_scale_ai_assisted_gene_expression_decoding.md","first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_003208","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a primary modeling paper on genetic data, not a review, benchmark/resource, or application wrapper. The provided text describes gene tissue-deconvolution, gene graph mapping, and genomic/methylation imputation, with no natural-language text component or text-bio bridge.","evidence_snippet":"\"decoding genetic data such as gene tissue-deconvolution, gene graph mapping and genomic imputation\"; \"deep generative models... infer missing methylation patterns\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_003208","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"The record reads like a broad overview of deep-learning uses for gene deconvolution, gene graph mapping, and imputation rather than a specific model paper. The only generative evidence is a general mention of 'deep generative models' and 'generative AI models,' without a described foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"'deep generative models, such as the Diffusion Denoise Probabilistic Model (DDPM)' and 'cutting-edge generative AI models' are mentioned in the introduction; the abstract discusses broad deep-learning applications rather than one model. Notes on text component and text-bio bridge were not assessed per instructions: not_assessed / not_assessed, and the generative phrase is 'deep generative models, such as the Diffusion Denoise Probabilistic Model (DDPM)' ."},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}}]
