the pretraining phase, rather than being limited to a single source. We also examine high-level tissue heatmaps to understand modality-specific attention patterns. As shown in Fig. 5, even in zero-shot settings, the multimodal model produces heatmaps closely resembling those of the fully trained model, reflecting strong generalization.\n\nFreeze vs. Fine-tune fusion layers. Another important issue to consider is the impact of whether to fine-tune the fusion layers on model performance. In our experiments, intuitively, for unimodal tasks involving only WSIs, we freeze the fusion layers, while in multimodal survival prediction tasks, we allow them to participate in training. To validate the rationale behind our intuition, we conduct ablation experiments (see Fig. 4). Results show that freezing fusion layers improves performance on unimodal tasks, while fine-tuning yields better results in multimodal settings. [truncated]","original_chars":1903},{"section_type":"discussion_conclusion","heading":"Conclusion","heading_level":2,"line_start":243,"line_end":326,"text":"In this article, we propose an any-to-any pretraining framework in the field of computational pathology. Using this framework, we pretrain an any-to-any model and validate its effectiveness through various multimodal downstream tasks. This framework can accept arbitrary modality combinations as input for training, addressing the issue of limited paired data in pathology. We find that with this pretraining framework, even for cancer types that the model has never encountered, fine-tuning can still achieve SOTA performance. While this paper focuses on the TCGA during the pretraining process, we believe that our framework can be extended to multiple pathology datasets to build a comprehensive foundation model.\n\n### References\n\n- [1] Jiabo Ma, Zhengrui Guo, Fengtao Zhou, Yihui Wang, Yingxue Xu, Yu Cai, Zhengjie Zhu, Cheng Jin, Yi Lin Xinrui Jiang, Anjia Han, et al. Towards a generalizable pathology foundation model via unified knowledge distillation. arXiv preprint arXiv:2407.18449 , 2024.\n- [2] Guillaume Jaume, Lukas Oldenburg, Anurag Vaidya, Richard J. Chen, Drew F.K. Williamson, Thomas Peeters, Andrew H. Song, and Faisal Mahmood. Transcriptomics-guided slide representation learning in computational pathology. In 2024 IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR) , pages 9632-9644, 2024.\n- [3] Yingxue Xu, Yihui Wang, Fengtao Zhou, Jiabo Ma, Shu Yang, Huangjing Lin, Xin Wang, Jiguang Wang, Li Liang, Anjia Han, et al. A multimodal knowledge-enhanced whole-slide pathology foundation model. [truncated]","original_chars":20425},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"Datasets and Tasks","heading_level":3,"line_start":155,"line_end":190,"text":"We use 6,850 WSI-omic-report pairs from TCGA as our pretraining dataset. To prevent data leakage, we selected four cancer types for validation in downstream tasks, while all data corresponding to the remaining 29 cancer types were included in the pretraining.\n\nTable 2: Results of cancer subtyping on 4 datasets. The best results are highlighted in bold , second best underlined.\n\n| Methods | BRACS | BRACS | PANDA | PANDA | TUPAC-16 | TUPAC-16 | UBC-OCEAN | UBC-OCEAN | Average | Average |\n|---------------|---------|----------|---------|----------|------------|------------|-------------|-------------|-----------|-----------|\n| Methods | AUC | F1 Score | AUC | F1 Score | AUC | F1 Score | AUC | F1 Score | AUC | F1 Score |\n| Mean Pooling | 0.804 | 0.446 | 0.951 | 0.734 | 0.665 | 0.455 | 0.970 | 0.845 | 0.877 | 0.620 |\n| Max Pooling | 0.776 | 0.427 | 0.919 | 0.642 | 0.688 | 0.548 | 0.971 | 0.813 | 0.870 | 0.608 |\n| WIKG [24] | 0.823 | 0.429 | 0.942 | 0.741 | 0.630 | 0.457 | 0.972 | 0.869 | 0.872 | 0.624 |\n| ABMIL [10] | 0.811 | 0.442 | 0.930 | 0.683 | 0.702 | 0.539 | 0.972 | 0.832 | 0.883 | 0.625 |\n| DS-MIL [62] | 0.790 | 0.439 | 0.903 | 0.591 | 0.707 | 0.538 | 0.976 | 0.830 | 0.874 | 0.600 |\n| TransMIL [14] | 0.729 | 0.346 | 0.916 | 0.644 | 0.656 | 0.540 | 0.966 | 0.803 | 0.853 | 0.584 |\n| CLAM-SB [13] | 0.781 | 0.434 | 0.930 | 0.688 | 0.687 | 0.453 | 0.972 | 0.811 | 0.873 | 0.597 |\n| ALTER(Ours) | 0.844 | 0.455 | 0.932 | 0.692 | 0.758 | 0.612 | 0.973 | 0.874 | 0.901 | 0.658 |\n\nTable 3: Results of gene mutation prediction on two datasets. [truncated]","original_chars":3513},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"Datasets","heading_level":2,"line_start":345,"line_end":387,"text":"#### TCGA Subsets 1 [70] for survival prediction.\n\nWe use four TCGA sub-datasets for unimodal survival analysis, each containing WSIs and gene expressions with corresponding survival outcome data:\n\n1. TCGA-UCEC: Comprises 480 WSI-omic pairs of uterine corpus endometrial carcinoma, representing various histological grades and disease stages. The dataset provides insights into morphological diversity in endometrial cancer and its prognostic implications.\n2. TCGA-STAD: Includes 317 WSI-omic pairs of stomach adenocarcinoma, covering multiple anatomical regions and histological subtypes of gastric cancer. The associated survival data enables investigation into morphology-outcome relationships.\n3. TCGA-KIRP: Comprises 284 WSI-omic pairs of kidney renal papillary cell carcinoma, representing a distinct subtype with papillary architecture and unique cellular patterns. The dataset facilitates comparative survival modeling across renal cancer subtypes.\n4. TCGA-KIRC: Contains 218 WSI-omic pairs of kidney renal clear cell carcinoma, characterized by clear cytoplasm and variable tumor grades. This dataset enables investigation into survival-relevant morphological features in renal cancers.\n\nAll TCGA datasets were digitized under standardized protocols and scanned at 40× magnification, providing high-resolution visualization of cellular and tissue structures. Each dataset is accompanied by rich clinical metadata, including survival outcomes, enabling the development and evaluation of computational models for prognostication based on histopathological features. [truncated]","original_chars":5923},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"Fusion Architecture","heading_level":3,"line_start":95,"line_end":108,"text":"To accommodate varying modality combinations during fusion, we design a two-stage fusion strategy within each universal sequence Transformer in Fig. 1 (a): (1) modalityshared fusion; (2) modality-specific decoupling. As shown in Fig. 2, we propose modality-specific mixture of experts (MoE) to decode structural characteristics of each modality from the fused latent space. This modality MoE architecture is particularly well-suited for CPath, as it enables the model to capture high-dimensional spatial features in WSIs, sparse tabular patterns in genomics, and semantic cues in clinical text.\n\nSpecifically, we employ modality-shared self-attention layers Φ( · ) to model interactions across modalities for the first stage. The shared attention matrix maps combinations of modalities into a unified latent space, Z ′ = Φ(Z) + Z , where Z = { H (3) , G (3) , T (1) } denotes the concatenation feature input and Z ′ = { H ′ , G ′ , T ′ } is the resulting output. In the next stage, we introduce modality-specific experts\n\nFigure 2: Universal sequence Transformer architecture of ALTER.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nto address the distinct statistical properties. These are defined as F ′′ = f F ( F ′ ) + F ′ , where F ∈ ( H,G,T ) represents the specific modality features, and f F is the corresponding expert module.\n\nNotably, the two-stage design inherently supports missing modality scenarios. [truncated]","original_chars":1804},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"Method","heading_level":2,"line_start":41,"line_end":152,"text":"### 3.1 Problem Formulation\n\nGiven a triplet ( W,G,R ) -where W denotes a WSI, G for the corresponding gene expressions, and R for the diagnostic report-we first decompose W into non-overlapping patches { p 1 , p 2 , . . . , p N h } and tokenize R into a sequence T ∈ R N t , with G ∈ R N g . Each patch is then processed using a pretrained feature extractor Θ . In this study, we adopt UNI [6], a foundation model for computational pathology, as the extractor. Formally, we obtain patch features h i = Θ( p i ) ∈ R d w , forming the feature bag H = { h 1 , h 2 , . . . , h N h } . As a result, the original triplet is converted into a serialized input ( H,G,T ) . Our objective is to learn a robust mapping f : X → Y in a weakly supervised manner, where X denotes the space of serialized triplets and Y is the latent representation space of the three modalities. The central challenge is to align the modalities effectively, ensuring that the latent space captures both modality-specific characteristics and cross-modal correlations.\n\n### 3.2 Modality Encoder\n\nAs shown in Fig. 1 (a), we use modality-specific encoders in the early stages of the model, which integrate intramodal information and prepare for subsequent fusion.\n\nDue to high input lengths, N h ≈ 20 , 000 , N g ≈ 10 , 000 , and N t ≈ 500 , direct multimodal fusion is computationally intensive. The concatenated sequence Y ∈ R ( N h + N g + N t ) × d incurs O (( N h + N g + N t ) 2 ) memory under self-attention [56]:\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded --> [truncated]","original_chars":11628}],"docling_markdown":"data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_001218.md","first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001218","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The record describes ALTER as a new any-to-any tri-modal pretraining framework integrating WSIs, genomics, and pathology reports. The model also supports report generation, so the text-bio bridge is substantive and central.","evidence_snippet":"\"integrates WSIs, genomics, and pathology reports\"; \"report generation\"; \"tokenize R into a sequence\"; \"semantic cues in clinical text\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001218","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The paper proposes ALTER as an any-to-any tri-modal pretraining framework and a foundation model for computational pathology. It mentions report generation as a downstream task, but the provided text does not clearly state a generative architecture or mechanism.","evidence_snippet":"\"any-to-any tri-modal pretraining framework\"; \"report generation\"; \"build a comprehensive foundation model\"; \"universal sequence Transformer architecture\"; \"modality-specific mixture of experts to decode structural characteristics\"; \"Foundation Models (FMs)\" in pathology is discussed as context, and the model is pretrainable on \"6,850 WSI-omic-report pairs\" with \"arbitrary modality combinations\" and \"strong generalization\" across tasks."},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"none","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}}]
